use std
into feature.amni

Imports: os, math, gfx, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf and gfx.scene3d (as s3d). This file is a module with no main. It also runs in the browser build, where an array gets a fixed region the first time it grows: every list that can hold more than a few hundred items (per texel, per grid cell, per vertex, per edge or per triangle) is sized once with resize to its final length, or to an upper bound with a separate count, and then written by index, never grown one push at a time. It adjusts single features of any loaded humanoid glTF or VRM (Holo, Dex or a model the user brings): eyeliner, lid and nose crease smoothing, cloth edge smoothing, and hiding or tinting single meshes. It finds the eyes and nose from the geometry itself, so no per-model landmarks are needed. All positions are the raw vertex positions in `gltf::verts` (floats 0..2 of each vertex, normal at `gltf::AT_NRM`, uv0 at `gltf::AT_UV0`, `gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS` floats per vertex); the model faces +z and y is up. "Front view" means dropping z. A primitive `gltf::prims[pp]` has the fields `vfirst`, `vcount`, `ifirst`, `icount` and `material`; its triangle corners are `pm.vfirst + (gltf::indices[pm.ifirst + 3 * q + k] as int)` for q below `pm.icount / 3` and k below 3. A mesh `gltf::meshes[m]` has `prims.first` and `prims.count`; a node has `mesh`; an asset has `nodes.first` and `nodes.count`; a material has `base` (float4), `base_tex.texture` and `name`.

Module vars: `var fe_hair = float4 { -1.0, -1.0, -1.0, 1.0 }` (strand hair color override r, g, b and brightness gain; an r below 0 keeps the model's own color), `var fe_do byte list, empty` (the name of a default hairstyle to wear in place of the model's own hair; empty keeps the model's hair), `var fe_shade byte list, empty` (the name of a default hair shade; empty keeps the color the model or the "hair" line gives), `var fe_c0 float3`, `var fe_c1 float3` (eyeball centers, fe_c0 the one with the smaller x), `var fe_r f32 = 0.0` (eyeball radius), `var fe_ipd f32 = 0.0` (distance between the two centers), `var fe_arc0 float3 list, empty` and `var fe_arc1 float3 list, empty` (upper lid margin of each eye, inner corner first), `var fe_px byte list, empty` (the module's own copy of the pixels of every image it may paint, one slot per image, sized once) and `var fe_at int list, empty` (offset of each image's slot in fe_px, -1 when it has none).

`fn fe_is_eye(nm text) bool` is true when the lowercase name contains "eye" and contains none of "lash", "brow", "occ", "shadow", "line", "lid", "spec", "high", "tear" and "socket".

`fn fe_skip(nm text) bool` is true when the lowercase name contains any of "eye", "lash", "brow", "hair", "teeth", "tooth", "tongue", "mouth" or "occ" (meshes that are never skin around the eyes).

`fn fe_mesh_name(asset int, pp int) text list`, for primitive index pp of a mesh of the asset, is that mesh's name (`gltf::text_of(gltf::meshes[m].name)`) followed by a space and the name of the primitive's material when it has one (`gltf::prims[pp].material >= 0`), so a name test sees both.

`fn fe_prims(asset int, eye bool) int list` lists every primitive index of every mesh referenced by a node of the asset (nodes from `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first`, count `.nodes.count`, mesh `gltf::nodes[n].mesh` when it is at least 0, prims from `gltf::meshes[m].prims.first`, count `.prims.count`), each primitive once; with eye true only those whose `fe_mesh_name` passes `fe_is_eye`, with eye false only those whose name passes neither `fe_is_eye` nor `fe_skip`.

`fn fe_sphere(ps float3 view) float4` fits a sphere to the points by linear least squares: unknowns a, b, c, k with `x² + y² + z² = 2ax + 2by + 2cz + k` for every point, solved through the 4 × 4 normal equations with Gaussian elimination and partial pivoting; returns center (a, b, c) in xyz and radius `sqrt(k + a² + b² + c²)` in w. When there are fewer than 4 points or a pivot is below 1e-12, return the mean of the points in xyz and their largest distance from that mean in w.

`fn fe_eyes(asset int) bool` finds the eyeballs. Collect the positions of every vertex of `fe_prims(asset, true)`; with fewer than 8 return false. Split them at their mean x into the smaller-x and larger-x halves and fit each with `fe_sphere`. Set fe_c0 and fe_c1 to the two centers (smaller x first), `fe_r` to the mean of the two radii and `fe_ipd` to the distance between the centers. Return true when fe_r is above 0 and fe_ipd is above twice fe_r; otherwise set fe_r to 0 and return false. Print "feature: eyes " fe_c0, fe_c1, " radius ", fe_r.

`fn fe_grid(prims int view, bx float4, n int, out f32 list by reference)` clears out and fills it with n × n cells covering the front-view box bx (min x, min y, max x, max y; cell (i, j) at index `j * n + i`, i along x, j along y), each the largest z of any triangle of the primitives covering the cell center, otherwise -1e9: for each triangle, only the cells inside its front-view bounding box, covered when the `fe_bary` weights are all at least -0.001, z from the weights.

`fn fe_arc(asset int, c float3, out float3 list by reference)` finds the upper lid margin of the eye whose eyeball is centered at c as the top edge of the visible eyeball seen from the front, so it works whatever the topology. Box bx is c plus and minus `1.6 * fe_r` in x and y; with n 128, `fe_grid` the skin `fe_prims(asset, false)` into gs and the eyes `fe_prims(asset, true)` into ge. A cell is open when its ge is above -1e8 and at least its gs. For each column i, its top open cell j (largest j); keep the point (x of the column center, y of the top edge of that cell, z the ge of that cell). Walk the columns from the inner side (toward x zero, so ascending i when c.x is above 0, otherwise descending), keeping only columns that have an open cell. Smooth the kept points three times, each interior point becoming a quarter of each neighbor plus half itself, then clear out and, when there are at least 2 points, push 24 points evenly spaced by length along the polyline of kept points (first and last included). Print "feature: lid arc ", the kept count, " -> ", out.len.

`fn fe_frame(asset int) bool` returns false when `fe_eyes(asset)` is false; otherwise fills fe_arc0 with `fe_arc(asset, fe_c0, ..)` and fe_arc1 with `fe_arc(asset, fe_c1, ..)` and returns true when both have at least 4 points.

`fn fe_reserve(asset int)` gives the module its own copy of every image it may paint, in one list sized once. With mx and my the mean of the x and of the y of fe_c0 and fe_c1, the front-view box is x from `mx - 1.6 * fe_ipd` to `mx + 1.6 * fe_ipd` and y from `my - 1.3 * fe_ipd` to `my + 0.9 * fe_ipd`. `fe_at` is resized to the number of images in `gltf::images`, every entry -1. For every primitive of `fe_prims(asset, false)` followed by `fe_prims(asset, true)` whose image `fe_prim_image(pp)` is at least 0 and still has `fe_at` -1, and that has at least one triangle whose front-view bounding box overlaps the box: its `fe_at` becomes the running total, and the total grows by `width * height * 4` of that image. Then `fe_px.resize(total, 0)` once. Then for every image with a slot: when its `coded.count` is above 0, `gltf::need_pixels(img)` and the source is `gltf::decoded`, otherwise the source is `gltf::image_pixels(img)`; copy the first `width * height * 4` bytes of the source into fe_px from the slot offset on, by index (copy only as many as the source has).

`fn fe_raw(img int) int` is the offset of image img's slot in `fe_px`: `fe_at[img]` when img is at least 0 and below `fe_at.len`, otherwise -1.

`fn fe_bary(a float3, b float3, c float3, x f32, y f32) float3` is the barycentric weights of point (x, y) in the 2D triangle a, b, c (x and y fields only); a degenerate triangle (area magnitude below 1e-12) gives (-1, -1, -1).

`fn fe_line(arc float3 view, w0 f32, w1 f32, wing f32, ang f32, lift f32, out f32 list by reference)` appends one eye's liner to out as rows of four floats (x, y, z, half width), all in front view meters. With n the arc length and `L` the front-view distance between its first and last points: for each arc point k, `u = k / (n - 1)`, width `w = (w0 + (w1 - w0) * u^1.5) * L`, the point moved up along the front-view normal of the arc (perpendicular to the neighbor difference, choosing the side with positive y) by `w * lift`, row (x, y, z, w / 2). Then the wing: with the last row's point q, `s` 1 when the last arc point has larger x than the first and -1 otherwise, direction `(s * cos(ang), sin(ang))` with ang in degrees, and length `wing * L`, append 8 rows at fractions 1/8 .. 8/8 along it, z the last row's z, half width going from the last row's half width to `0.004 * L` linearly.

`fn fe_dist(lv f32 view, first int, count int, x f32, y f32) f32` is the smallest signed front-view distance from (x, y) to the capsule chain of rows first .. first + count of lv: for each consecutive pair of rows, project the point onto the segment (clamped to its ends), take the distance to that point minus the half width interpolated at the same fraction; keep the minimum. Return 1e6 when count is below 2.

`fn fe_upload(img int)` refreshes image img on the GPU after its pixels changed in its slot: nothing when `fe_raw(img)` is -1. With base that offset and n the image's `width * height * 4`: when the image's `coded.count` is 0 and its `pixels.count` is at least n, copy the n slot bytes back into `gltf::pixels` from `gltf::images[img].pixels.first` on, by index (so later readers of the image see the edits). Then when img is below `s3d::tex_srgb.len` and `s3d::tex_srgb[img].id` is not 0, `gfx::update_texture(s3d::tex_srgb[img], fe_px[base..base + n])` then `gfx::generate_mips(s3d::tex_srgb[img])`.

`fn fe_blur(a f32 list by reference, w int, h int, r int)` replaces the w × h grid a with its box blur of radius r (window 2r + 1), done as a horizontal pass then a vertical pass, each a running window average with coordinates clamped to the grid edges.

`fn fe_clean(asset int, rg f32 view, amt f32, rad int, keep f32)` removes painted dark lines (lash shadows, liner, creases drawn into the skin texture) inside the regions by filling them from the surrounding skin. For every image used as the base color texture of a primitive of `fe_prims(asset, false)` that has at least one triangle near the regions (as defined next; only then take its slot offset base from `fe_raw`, skipping the image when that is -1; width tw, height th; its texel bytes r, g, b, a sit at `fe_px[base + (y * tw + x) * 4]` onward, and every read or write of texel bytes in this block and in any helper it uses goes to `fe_px`, never `gltf::pixels`): a per-texel weight cm, zero to start; for every such primitive using that image and every triangle whose front-view bounding box comes within `0.1 * fe_ipd` of any region row's box (center plus and minus radius), for every texel center inside the triangle's uv0 bounding box (uv times the image size) with `fe_bary` weights on the uv triangle all at least -0.02, the surface point p from the weights and `cm = max(cm, fe_weight(rg, p.x, p.y, 4))`. Skip the image when no texel got a weight above 0. Work on the sub-rectangle of texels with weight above 0, widened by `3 * rad` on every side and clamped to the image. In it (every per-texel list here, lu, mk, bl, bm and the three fill channels, is sized once with resize to the sub-rectangle size and written by index): lu is r + g + b of each texel; mask mk starts at 1; four passes: bl the blur of `lu * mk` and bm the blur of mk (both `fe_blur` with rad), and in the first three passes mk becomes 1 where `lu >= keep * bl / max(bm, 0.0001)`, otherwise 0. Then per channel the blur of `channel * mk` divided by `max(bm, 0.0001)` is the fill color. Each texel with cm above 0, mk below 0.5 and bm at least 0.05 blends toward the fill color by `min(cm * amt, 1)` (bytes clamped to 0..255). Count them; `fe_upload` the image. Print "feature: cleaned " and the count.

`fn fe_liner(asset int, w0 f32, w1 f32, wing f32, ang f32, lift f32, dark f32, st f32)` paints a smooth liner into the base color textures of the skin around the eyes and of the eyes. Return when `fe_r` is 0 or st is not above 0. Build `lv` with `fe_line` for fe_arc0 then fe_arc1 (remember each eye's first row and count). Front-view box of all rows widened by `0.2 * fe_r` on every side. Depth grid: `fe_grid` over both `fe_prims(asset, false)` and `fe_prims(asset, true)` joined, n 256, over that box. Then for every primitive of both lists whose material has a base color texture (`gltf::materials[m].base_tex.texture >= 0`, image `gltf::textures[tx].image`, its slot offset base from `fe_raw` (skip the primitive when that is -1; texel bytes are read and written only in `fe_px` at `base + (y * width + x) * 4`), size `im.width` × `im.height`): skip triangles whose front-view bounding box misses the widened box; otherwise for every texel center (x + 0.5, y + 0.5) inside the triangle's uv0 bounding box (uv times the image size), weights from `fe_bary` on the uv triangle all at least -0.02: the surface point p from the weights; for skin primitives only, skip it when its grid cell's z is above `p.z + 0.06 * fe_r` (hidden behind other skin); eyeball texels are never skipped, because the ones right under the lid edge must be painted and the lid hides the rest (the app turns the head, not the eyes); `d` the smaller of `fe_dist` for both eyes at (p.x, p.y); alpha `(1 - smoothstep(-k, k, d)) * st` with `k = 0.012 * fe_r`; when alpha is above 0 blend the texel's rgb bytes toward dark (a byte value) by alpha. Print "feature: liner texels " and the count. Look up an image's offset (`fe_raw`) only when the first of its triangles passes the box test, and finally `fe_upload` only the images that got at least one painted texel.

`fn fe_weight(rg f32 view, x f32, y f32, col int) f32` is the region weight at front-view point (x, y) for the regions rg (rows of six floats: center x, center y, radius x, radius y, crease amount, edge amount): over the rows, with `dx = (x - cx) / rx`, `dy = (y - cy) / ry` and `dd = sqrt(dx² + dy²)`, the largest of `row[col] * (1 - smoothstep(0.5, 1, dd))`; when col is below 0 the largest of `1 - smoothstep(0.85, 1.7, dd)` instead (any influence at all). Zero when there are no rows.

`fn fe_soften(asset int, prims int view, rg f32 view, it int, fill f32)` smooths skin in front-view regions. rg holds rows of six floats: center x, center y, radius x, radius y (meters), crease amount, edge amount. For each primitive: for each vertex at (x, y) in front view, crease weight `wa = fe_weight(rg, x, y, 4)` and edge weight `wb = fe_weight(rg, x, y, 5)`; keep vertices where `fe_weight(rg, x, y, -1)` is above 0. Every per-vertex list (positions, weights, kept flags, weld targets, sums) is sized once with resize to the vertex count, and every per-edge list once to six entries per triangle with a separate count, then written by index. Weld kept vertices at the same position (squared distance below 1e-12) to the first one. Build the triangle edge list among kept welded vertices (neighbors). A welded pair is a rim edge when some edge between original (unwelded) vertex indices with those two welded ends is used by exactly one triangle: that is a hole edge or a texture seam, and lid margins are often a seam, so they are smoothed as curves. Count raw edges in a map keyed by the two original indices, smaller first. Then `it * 2` passes, `lam` 0.5 on even passes and -0.53 on odd ones: for each welded vertex with neighbors, `di` = mean of neighbors minus itself, `ni` = its normal times dot(di, normal); a vertex on a rim edge moves by (mean of its rim-edge neighbors minus itself) times `lam * wb`; any other vertex moves by `ni * wa * la + di * wb * lam`, where `la` is lam on even passes and `lam * (1 - fill)` on odd passes (fill 1 lets creases fill in instead of springing back). Copy each welded position to its duplicates, then turn each normal by the change of the triangle normal around it (`unit(n + unit(after) - unit(before))` with before and after the area-weighted vertex normals of its triangles). Print "feature: softened " and the moved vertex count.

`fn fe_regions(kind text, out f32 list by reference)` appends the automatic regions in meters, using fe_c0, fe_c1, the midpoint m of the two and `i = fe_ipd`, mirroring x for the larger-x eye (offsets toward the outer side are negative for fe_c0 and positive for fe_c1). For "lids", per eye center c: the crease band at (c.x - 0.056 i toward the outer side, c.y + 0.076 i) radii (0.316 i, 0.112 i) with amounts (1, 0), and the lid edge at (c.x, c.y - 0.034 i) radii (0.304 i, 0.117 i) with amounts (0, 1). For "nose": two rows at (m.x - 0.236 i, m.y - 0.613 i) and (m.x + 0.236 i, m.y - 0.613 i), radii (0.183 i, 0.281 i), amounts (1, 0).

`fn fe_mesh_prims(asset int, name text) int list` lists the primitives of every mesh of the asset's nodes whose mesh name equals name, each once.

`fn fe_hide(asset int, name text)` sets `gltf::nodes[n].mesh = -1` for every node of the asset whose mesh name equals name.

`fn fe_tint(asset int, name text, c float3)` multiplies `.base.x`, `.y` and `.z` of the material of every primitive of `fe_mesh_prims(asset, name)` by c's fields (each material once).

`fn fe_amounts(rg f32 list by reference, a f32, b f32)` multiplies the fifth float of every row by a and the sixth by b.

`pub fn feature_setup(path text, asset int) bool` reads the per-model look file at path (`read_file` into a byte list; missing file: return false) and applies its lines in order. Each line is a feature word followed by numbers or a mesh name, separated by spaces; blank lines and lines starting with "#" are skipped; unknown words are ignored. Numbers parse with `parse_flt`; a missing number takes the default listed here.
- "liner st w0 w1 wing ang lift dark" (defaults 1, 0.05, 0.185, 0.3, 22, 0.075, 18): when `fe_frame(asset)` holds, `fe_liner(asset, w0, w1, wing, ang, lift, dark, st)`.
- "lids crease edge passes fill" (defaults 1, 1, 25, 1): with the "lids" regions scaled by `fe_amounts(crease, edge)`, `fe_soften` over `fe_prims(asset, false)`.
- "clean amount radius keep" (defaults 1, 7, 0.85): `fe_clean` with the "lids" regions (when `fe_frame(asset)` holds), radius as a whole number.
- "nose amount passes fill" (defaults 1, 25, 1): the "nose" regions scaled by `fe_amounts(amount, 0)`, `fe_soften` over `fe_prims(asset, false)`.
- "smooth mesh passes" (default passes 20): `fe_soften(asset, fe_mesh_prims(asset, mesh), one row (0, 0, 1e5, 1e5, 0, 1), passes, 0)`.
- "hide mesh": `fe_hide`.
- "tint mesh r g b" (defaults 1, 1, 1): `fe_tint`.
- "hair r g b gain" (defaults -1, -1, -1, 1): set fe_hair to `float4 { r, g, b, gain }`.
- "do name": the name is every word after the first, joined by single spaces (style names such as "long waves" have two words); fe_do becomes those bytes.
- "shade name": the same for fe_shade.
The eyeballs are found once, the first time a line needs them; the lid margins are found again (`fe_frame`) right before each "liner" line, so they follow any smoothing done by earlier lines. Before the first "clean" or "liner" line does its work, when `fe_at` is empty, `fe_reserve(asset)` (after the eyeballs are found). Return true.
